Z||000000||SUPERDUTY TT FIERO||12-18-2001||04:08 AM||||HELP ANYONE I WAS DRIVING MY FIERO ONE DAY AND I WAS IN DRIVING AND ON THE WAY HOME THE STICK WAS WORKING FINE AND WHEN I WOKE UP AND THE CLUTCH WILL NOT DISENGAGE IS THIS BECAUSE THE TRANNY RAN LOW ON OIL?? DO I NEED A NEW TRANSMISSION?? THE CLUTCH IS ONLY 5 MONTHS OLD AND IT WORKS FINE WHEN IM IN GEAR BUT IT JUST WONT DISENGAGE!! I CAN START IN ANY GEAR AND IT WORKS ONLY IN THAT GEAR AND WHEN I TRY TO SHIFT IT STRATCHES WHEN I ATTEMPT TO CHANGE GEARS AND WILL NOT GO IN PLEASE HELP ME I FEEL LOST WITHOUT MY FIERO!!!! I HAVE CHANGED ALL LINES, MASTER CYLINDER AND SLAVE CYLINDER SO I KNOW ITS INTERNAL BUT IS THE CLUTCH ASSEMBLY BAD OR IS IT THE TRANNY<BR>THANKS TO ALL THAT REPLY<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||12-18-2001||01:12 PM||||(You *did* say this was a manual transmission, right?)<P>I'm not sure why the transmission running low on oil would cause the clutch release system to malfunction... they're two separate things. The oil in the tranny doesn't have anything to do with the clutch plates, release bearing fork, or the hydraulic system connected to the clutch pedal... ::scratching head::<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||12-18-2001||02:43 PM||||You have likely fried the innards of your tranny.  The clutch may feel OK when you push in on the pedal, but if the internal forks and whatnot are broken/melted/whatever you will not be able to shift gears at all.<P>I know from experience.......I sold a 5-sp. '88 Formula to a friend of mine and we decided to drive to Las Vegas.  He drove his Formula (with his girlfriend) and I drove mine.  Evidently the axle seals developed a leak and he was clueless about it......we got halfway up Vail Pass (elevation 14K feet) and he said it kept popping out of gear. We pulled into a rest area and checked the oil level....it was bone dry.  Luckily I had some motor oil in the trunk.  We filled it up and attempted to leave again, but it was stuck in 2nd gear!  <P>Long story short......he had literally melted the tranny internally.  It sounds like you have done the same, although maybe not as dramatic as driving up a big mountain.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>What tranny do you have?  I have an Isuzu 5-sp and a Muncie 4-sp., so if you need one cheap I can help you out.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-18-2001||03:57 PM||||Ouch!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||westtexas||12-18-2001||06:20 PM||||I bought an 86' GT, Muncie 4 speed, that will stick in third gear. I found that I had only a half pint of fluid in the transmission. I'm still driving it but short shifting between second and fourth. If I get on it hard in third it will stick every time. One good thing about Muncies, they aren't that scarce or valuable compared to a Getrag.<P>Ed Parks has instructions on his web page for changing out the transmission without pulling the engine. Good luck!<P>Robert||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-19-2001||10:29 AM||||Do you have a good pedal.....firm?  Just on the off chance that the clutch stuck to the flywheel, put the car in 4th gear and get a friend to help you rock it.  This does not happen often but it does happen.  Worth a shot!<P>phil pettus<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_4.JPG"> ||||reg||1
